 game. I have to avoid making a costly mistake.\n\n**Final Decision**\n\nI have decided to check (0). It\'s the most conservative play. It maximizes the probability of winning the hand without risking too much.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: You hold Two Pair (Eights and Twos), which is a very strong hand in heads-up play. The opponent showed aggression on the flop but checked the Turn card (10c). The 10 is an overcard to your eights, meaning if the opponent has a 10, they have a higher pair, or if they have 10-10/8-8/2-2, they have a set. However, it is more likely they have a weaker hand or a draw. Checking back allows you to control the pot size, avoid being check-raised by a superior hand, and potentially induces the opponent to bluff or call a bet on the River with a worse hand.\n\nReason: By checking, you ensure you see the River card for free, keeping the pot manageable and disguising the strength of your two pair. This maximizes your chance of winning the hand at showdown while minimizing the risk of losing a large pot to a set or higher two pair.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
