cker (8) is low, and any opponent holding a card higher than an 8 (9, 10, J, Q) or connecting with the Ace or King dominates you. Although the opponent has checked multiple times, showing weakness, the board texture (A, K, K) is dangerous. Bluffing is risky because the opponent is unlikely to fold a King or an Ace, and might even call with a pocket pair or high card. Since you have no draw potential (no flush or straight draws), the most prudent play is to check back to see the River for free without inflating the pot.\n\nReason: You have poor showdown value and no drawing potential. Checking minimizes risk and preserves chips for better opportunities, avoiding the danger of bluffing into a board that heavily favors high cards.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
